{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Importantly however, high rates of poor mental health are common in clinical staff working in acute medicine generally [3-5] ... Recently, a meta-analysis by Kisely et al. [6] reported that healthcare workers in direct contact with affected patients had an approximately 1.71-1.74 odds ratio of reporting acute mental health problems",
      "reason": "The introduction describes the current state of knowledge, including existing evidence of poor mental health in acute medical staff and the prior meta-analysis findings."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "estimating the additional impact of epidemic and pandemic response, not solely the extent of poor mental health, is also important in guiding decisions to protect staff mental health ... Consequently, we report a rapid review and meta-analysis ... to understand the potential impact on mental health and to inform policy on supporting staff during the current COVID-19 pandemic.",
      "reason": "The importance and need for the review are explicitly explained: estimating the additional burden and informing policy during COVID-19."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Recently, a meta-analysis by Kisely et al. [6] reported that healthcare workers in direct contact with affected patients had an approximately 1.71-1.74 odds ratio ... traditional meta-analyses are only likely to give a snapshot of the current state of the evidence. Living systematic reviews or meta-analyses are a new innovation that allow estimates to be updated as new evidence emerges [7, 8] but have rarely been used to date.",
      "reason": "A similar prior systematic review (Kisely et al.) is identified and cited, and the need for the current review (living, updated meta-analysis) is explained."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This is a review of prevalence/mental health outcomes in clinical staff, not a review of intervention effects, so no mechanism of action needs describing."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review is not of complex interventions; it examines mental health burden in staff during epidemics, so a logic model is not required."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 92
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Consequently, we report a rapid review and meta-analysis that forms the basis of a living meta-analysis of the mental health of clinical staff dealing with epidemics and pandemics of high-risk infectious diseases... to understand the potential impact on mental health and to inform policy on supporting staff during the current COVID-19 pandemic.",
      "reason": "The review objective is explicitly stated in the Introduction (and echoed in the abstract: 'we aimed to estimate the additional burden of work involving high exposure to infected patients'), specifying the population, exposures, outcomes, and purpose of the review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "two types of meta-analytic estimates were possible: a comparison of risk ratios for studies that compared above-cut off scoring between high- and low-exposure groups, and meta-analysis for differences in mean scale scores between high and low exposures",
      "reason": "The planned comparison (high- vs low-exposure work on anxiety, PTSD, and depression outcomes) is described, but it is not formulated using an explicit PICO framework; exposure definitions are given in Methods rather than as a structured statement of comparisons."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 85
}

{
